A||||5||fierose||Click... Click... fuel pump?||||5||
Z||000000||fierose||07-07-2000||01:54 AM||||I (85SE) was just wondering if you guys could help me out.  I have this clicking sound coming from my engine.  Upon further examination using my dowel rod it seems like the sound is coming from the fuel return line coming out of my throttle body.  You can hear the sound at the fuel feed where the fuel filter is located but it's not as strong.  Everywhere else in the engine bay I listened for the sound I couldn't really hear it.  I'm wondering if your fuel pump might make this sound on it's last leg, I did run my tank dry a few times when I first got the car 12 months ago. I then realized 1/4 tank meant go get gas.  Or maybe something in the TBI that could make this sound?  I looked in to checking my fuel pressure but the fitting and gages for TBI were like $50.  I was also wondering if a worn fuel pump could cause a loss of performance.  Any suggestions you guys have would be greatly appreciated.  It's an everyday driver so I don't won't to left on the side of the road.  Thanks.  Cliff you tha man!<P>J.D.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierose||07-07-2000||01:56 AM||||oops double post<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000002||My7Fieros||07-07-2000||02:37 AM||||Well, what kind of sound are you hearing? If its a clicking sound, its more than likely your fuel injectors doing their job. Its supposed to make that sound. If its a noticable loud clicking, prob a manifold leak.||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||07-07-2000||01:45 PM||||Your fuel system will pressureize for 2 seconds when you first turn the key on.  Without starting the engine, just turn the key on, and the fuel system pressurizes itself so it has enough pressure to start.  Is that the sound your hearing?||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||07-07-2000||03:02 PM||||A little clarification might help us help you: Does the clicking sound occur with engine turned off?  Does it  happen with ignition turned on or off?<P>If you have an exhaust manifold leak, the clicking will occur only with engine running, and clicking will speed up/ slow down proportional to engine speed (rpm's).    ||||reg||5
Z||000005||fierose||07-08-2000||01:17 AM||||Sorry, I was a little vague.  The sound is there with the engine running.  I haven't had anyone rev the engine while i've listened but I'm guessing the clicking sound won't change too much with rpm.  The sound is definitely coming from or being transferred to the fuel return line somehow.  The sound is very consistent and sounds like something very mechanical, almost like metal hitting together very rapidly.  It almost borders on a very rapid clicking sound and almost a rattle.  It's kind of hard to explain but it definitely not coming directly from the engine.  I'll have to check the manifolds and the egr pipe to remove them as a possiblility.  I'll also check to see if it varies according to rpm.  I don't have any error codes except the IAC, after it idles low for 30s if my memory serves me right.  It might be a normal sound coming from the car that has gotten worse for some reason.  Is there anything in the throttle body that could do this?  Thanks for your help I'll check in on it a little more.||||reg||6
